@@How this problem works

\ni In order to see if a power of an element lies in a subgroup,
we add the generators of the subgroup to the relators of the containing
group and check to see if the image of the element is of finite order
in the resultant quotient group.

\ni For a general discussion, see
@General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@.

@@How this problem works


\ni In order to determine whether the elements represented by two
words are equal, we compute their canonical decompositions. The
elements are equal if and only if their canonical decompositions
coincide. For a general discussion see 
@General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@.



@@How this problem works

@@ABELIAN

The automorphism can be presented as 

        a_i -> w_i = a_1^u_i_1 ... a_n^u_i_n , 

where a_i are generators of the group and u_i_j are integers.
The word v = a_1^x_1 ... a_n^x_n is a fixed point if the
automorphism maps it to itself i.e. v -> v.
So we substitute a_i in v by w_i and resulting word must
equal to v. The problem can be reduced to finding solutions 
for the system of equations

        u_1_1 x_1 + ... + u_n_1 x_n = x_1
        
        :
     
        u_i_n x_1 + ... + u_n_n x_n = x_n

From all solutions we take only basic ones and words
a_1^x_1 ... a_n^x_n generate the subgroup of fixed points.

\ni See also @General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@ for a general discussion of abelian groups.   
  

    @@How this problem works


\ni Notice that an epimorphism of a finitely generated abelian group 
is an automorphism, since a finitely generated abelian group is never
isomorphic to any of its proper factor groups. Consequently, in order 
to determine if an endomorphism is an automorphism, it suffices to
check whether an endomorphism of a finitely generated abelian group is
an automorphism, it suffices to check whether it is an epimorphism,
i.e., whether the image of the given group coincides with the group
itself. This is checked by means of an existing algorithm.


\ni For a general discussion of general finitely generated abelian groups,
see @General discussion@General_abelian_groups::general discussion@

@@How this problem works

@@ABELIAN

\ni We need only check whether the images of the generators of the given group
generate the group, which we can do on appealing to the @algorithm which determines whether elements of a group generate it@probs/AbelianIsSGEqualToTheGroup::ABELIAN@
@@How this problem works

\ni We compute the invariants of the given group and those of
its image. The given homomorphism is an isomorphism if and only
these two sets of invariants coincide.

\ni For a general discussion, see
@General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@.

@@How this problem works

\ni We compute the canonical decompositions of the given group and its
image. The homomorphism involved is a monomorphism if and only if these 
decompositions are the same.

\ni For a general discussion, see
@General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@.




@@How this problem works

@@ABELIAN

\ni We compute the canonical decomposition for the group
and then use the Kunneth formula, which gives the integral 
homology of a direct product of two groups in terms of tensor 
products of the integral homology of the factors.

\ni See also @General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@ for a general discussion of abelian groups. @@How this problem works


\ni In order to see if two subgroups are isomorphic, we compute their 
canonical decompositions. The subgroups are isomorphic if and only if
they have the same canonical decompositions.

\ni For a general discussion, see
@General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@.



%@@How this problem works
@@FP



@@ABELIAN

\ni The given subgroup coincides with the group if and only
if the factor group of the overgroup by the subgroup is trivial.
Here we simply use the algorithm for deciding whether an abelian
group is trivial to solve this problem.

\ni For a general discussion see
@General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@

@@NILPOTENT

@@FREE

@@AP_FREE

@@AP_FREE_CYCLIC

@@SMALL_CANCELLATION

@@ONE_RELATOR








@@How this problem works

\ni The isolator of the  given subgroup is the pre-image of the torsion
subgroup of the factor group of the given group by the subgroup. The torsion
subgroup is computed using an existing algorithm.

\ni For a general discussion see 
@General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@

@@How this problem works
@@FP

@@ABELIAN

\ni We compute the canonical decompositions of the given groups, which
are isomorphic if and only if these decompositions are the same.

\ni For a general discussion, see
@General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@

@@NILPOTENT

