@@How this problem works

@@ABELIAN

\ni The given group is decomposed into a direct product of finitely
many infinite cyclic groups aand finitely many finite cyclic groups. 
Its  order is infinite if there is at least one  infinite cyclic
factor; otherwise it is finite and its order is the product of the 
orders of the finite cyclic factors in the decomposition.

\ni For a discussion of general finitely generated abelian groups,
see @General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@














@@How this problem works

\ni In order to compute the p-height of an element, we compute
its primary decomposition. If the element is of finite order
prime to p, its p-height is infinite. Otherwise, its p-height
can be read off from is primary decomposition. It is enough to
note that if a is an element of either infinite order or of p-power
order, then the p-height of a^n is the largest power of p dividing 
n, whenever a^n is not equal to 1.

\ni For a general discussion see 
@General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@.








































































@@How this problem works

\ni In order to see if an element is a proper power, we compute
a maximal root of the element. This comes equipped with a positive
integer giving us the power of the maximal root that coincides with
the given element. If that power is greater than 1, the element is
a proper power, otherwise it is not.

\ni For a general discussion, see
@General_abelian_groups@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@


@@How this problem works

@@FP

@@ABELIAN

Every finitely generated abelian group can be decomposed into a
direct product of finitely many infinite cyclic groups and finitely
many cyclic groups of finite order. Since each such group is given
by finitely many generators and finitely many defining relators 
in the category of abelian groups, i.e., as a quotient of a free
abelian group of finite rank by a subgroup generated by finitely
many elements, such a decomposition can be obtained by Gaussian
Elimination. This decomposition can be arranged so that the finite
cyclic groups are all of prime-power order, arranged 
in groups. The first group involves the cyclic factors where
the prime appearing is the largest of all and the cyclic
factors are arranged in decreasing order. The second group
involves the next biggest prime and so on. This decomposition
is usually termed the primary decomposition of the given abelian
group.


