@@FP

\ni There is no general and effective method for solving the triviality
problem for all finitely presented groups. However, given a specific 
finite presentation of a finitely presented group, it may be possible
to determine whether the given group is trivial. We simply invoke the 
procedure for deciding whether a given word in a finitely presented
group is trivial and apply it to determine whether the generators of
the given group are trivial (see @The word problem@probs/WordProblem::FP@).

\ni For a general discussion of finitely presented groups, see
@General finitely presented groups@General_finitely_presented_groups::
General discussion@

@@ABELIAN

\ni The given group is decomposed into a direct product of cyclic groups.
It is trivial if this decomposition consists only of a single cyclic
group whose order is 1.

\ni For a discussion of general finitely generated abelian groups,
see @General discussion@General_abelian_groups::General discussion@

@@NILPOTENT

\ni  A nilpotent group is trivial if and only if its abelianization is
trivial. Since we can decide whether a finitely generated abelian group 
is trivial, this means that we can decide whether a finitely generated 
nilpotent group is trivial (cf. @Is a finitely generated abelian group trivial?@probs/IsTrivialProblem::ABELIAN@).

@@FREE

@@AP_FREE

@@AP_FREE_CYCLIC












@@How this problem works

@@FP

\ni We compute the low central series for both groups

G_0 < G_1 < G_2 < ...

and 

H_0 < H_1 < H_2 < ...

Then we find LCS quotients i.e. the i-th quotients are
G_i+1 / G_i and H_i+1 / H_i. These groups are abelian,
so we can check them on isomorphism 
( see for details 
@Isomorphism for abelian groups@probs/AbelianIsomProblem::ABELIAN@ 
). If i-th quotients aren't isomorphic then the given groups
aren't isomorphic, but if all quotients are isomorphic the
algorithm can't give an exact answer.

\ni For a general discussion of finitely presented groups, see
@General_finitely_presented_groups@General_fp_gps::General discussion@    
@@FP

\ni The implementation of the method of determining whether a group has
a finite rewriting system as well as that of Knuth-Bendix used here is 
that of Charles Sims.

\ni Every finite presentation can be re-expressed as a set of rules.
The method of Knuth-Bendix takes the given rules and adds new ones
to them. These rules allow one to successively reduce a given word
to a word that cannot be reduced any further. If all reductions lead
to the same word, then the set of rules that has been obtained so far
is termed confluent and the given group is said to have a confluent
rewriting system. On the other hand if two reductions lead to different 
words, one adds an additional rule to the existing set, one which implies
that the greater of these two words (in the lexicographic ordering) implies
the smaller. If the process eventually terminates, i.e., all reductions
of any given word lead to the same one, then we have arrived at a confluent
rewriting system. For a detailed discussion of this topic, see the book
by C.S. Sims entitled Computations in finitely presented groups.

\ni For a general discussion of finitely presented groups, see
@General finitely presented groups@General_fp_gps::General discussion@

@@ABELIAN

The given group is decomposed into a direct product of cyclic groups.
It is trivial if this decomposition consists only of a single cyclic
group whose order is 1.

For a discussion of general finitely generated abelian groups,
see @General discussion@General_abelian_groups::general discussion@

@@NILPOTENT

@@FREE

@@AP_FREE

@@AP_FREE_CYCLIC

@@SMALL_CANCELLATION

@@ONE_RELATOR

